WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:06.400
Good morning John and thank you, ever so much, for your truth or fail on animal sex

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.460
'cause boy, I couldn't have lived without knowing that stuff.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:13.680
But you missed the most fascinating thing about the traumatic insemination of bed bugs!

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.620
How dare you! Some bed bugs stab their sperm through the thorax of a female to inseminate her

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.760
and that is weird and gross and kind of horrible BUT...

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.880
the men also do it to each other!

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:30.280
Not because they're, you know, just like willy nilly stabbing everything they can with their sperm spikes,

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:35.500
but because, and this is crazy, they are actually shoving their sperm

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.780
into the other male to mix their sperms together so if that male is successful

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.480
at inseminating a female there is a chance that the sperm that actually ends up

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:49.820
fertilizing her eggs will be the original sperm stabbing male. *shudders*

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.320
Actual Video Topic

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:56.440
You may have heard that small particles function both as particles and waves.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.820
And it's difficult to understand how weird this is,

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.360
but there's a great video that you can watch that will actually explain it to you

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:07.340
and then your mind will melt and you will never be able to function correctly as a human again.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.660
You should watch that video; there's a link to it right here.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.120
There's also a link to it in the description.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.800
But here's the thing: electrons are very simple.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.740
They're as elementary as you can get, but even electrons

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.120
are difficult to categorize. Wave? Particle? Both?

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.400
We are orders of magnitude more complicated than electrons

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.320
and yet we still attempt to categorize ourselves and each other.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.560
Today is National Coming Out Day in the UK and it was yesterday in the US

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.520
So let's get this out of the way.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.600
When I was a younger man I was a bit of an electron

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.660
I wasn't sure if I was a particle or if I was a wave

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.080
unless someone was observing in which case I was definitely a particle.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:50.160
Some people know if they're gay or straight by the time they're five years old.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.600
But I kinda think that those people are the exception rather than the rule,

00:01:53.600 --> 00:02:00.560
and by the time that I reached puberty I knew that being gay came along, at least theoretically, with several social cues.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:01.260
For example:

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:05.000
If someone was a little more flamboyant than usual,

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.540
if someone was a big fan of ice skating and really loved his collection of stuffed animals,

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:14.720
and hung out almost exclusively with girls but in a non romantic way.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.860
Those social cues came along with homosexuality and so

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.540
I thought I might be gay. And this was scary for me because I really didn't want to be different.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:27.360
I remember very specifically ice skating while listening to The Village People

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:34.240
and, this is just idiotic but, you know, I'm a kid. I'm twelve years old and I'm worried about being different

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.580
and I'm like I like ice skating to The Village People so much!

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:39.980
This is a problem!

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.840
And it sounds funny but it isn't. It's not funny for a lot of reasons.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:46.120
One, because someone can be so afraid of what they might be.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.860
Two, because just because you like ice skating to The Village People doesn't mean

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.500
anything about your sexual orientation. So it turns out that I'm not gay.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.740
What I am is kind of effeminate. I'm just sort of a less guyish kind of guy.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:02.740
Sexual orientation is very complicated and it's rarely 100% in either direction.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.560
I'm like a Kinsey one or two heterosexual.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.300
It's hard to recognize that especially because that makes a mess of all our assumptions about what

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.440
gender roles and sexual orientation are supposed to mean about people.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.800
If an electron were a person I would wanna know a heck of a lot more about that electron

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:25.260
than whether it was a particle or a wave before I made a judgement

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:28.360
about what kind of person/electron it was.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:31.880
Here's my message on National Coming Out Day in the UK:

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:36.060
Be honest with yourself. Be honest with the people that you love.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.480
Be honest with yourself even if you're not quite sure who you are,

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:45.080
because most of us are unsure of who we are, I mean, that's a very difficult and huge question.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:49.100
So just try and be who you are and be as honest with yourself as you can be.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:53.240
Unless you are a bed bug, in which case, just STOP!

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.200
John, I'll see you on Friday.

